# Landing persona + visual architecture — invariants

## Why this rule exists

The Master Brief (`ResearchOne — Market, Competitive, and GTM Master Brief (May 2026).md`)
identifies four buyer tribes (OSINT / investigative journalism, UAP /
paranormal disclosure, academic researchers, patent / IP) plus a
default audience. The strategic doc (`Strategic Architectural and
Go-To-Market Blueprint`) prescribes persona-adaptive landing copy and
a lab-notebook visual aesthetic. Both surfaces are exposed-to-the-world
brand decisions; mistakes here are visible publicly, undermine
PolicyOne credibility (a tracking-heavy landing page contradicts the
"reason over recall, no institutional surveillance" doctrine), and
are difficult to roll back without harming SEO and ad campaigns.

This rule exists so the persona layer does not become a surveillance
vector, the visual layer does not regress accessibility, and both
compose cleanly with the WO-W animated pipeline and WO-U cost
analytics that ship adjacent to them.

## Invariants

### I-1. Persona detection is 100% client-side

`personaResolver.resolve()` reads ONLY: URL search params, `document.referrer`,
URL pathname, and (where present) a session-scoped `sessionStorage`
cache. It NEVER reads cookies, never fingerprints the client, never
issues a network call, and never passes referrer text to the backend.

The Master Brief's positioning depends on credibility with privacy-
sensitive audiences (OSINT journalists, IC analysts, UAP researchers).
Sending raw referrer URLs to our backend would be the same kind of
"institutional surveillance" we critique in our own doctrine. Don't.

### I-2. Persona ID is the only thing that ever crosses the wire

If WO-V's optional analytics endpoint is enabled, the ONLY field that
goes to the backend is the resolved persona id (`'osint' | 'uap' |
'academic' | 'patent' | 'default'`) plus the path that was visited.
No referrer string. No UTM bag. No timestamp finer than minute. No
user identifier.

The analytics table is intentionally append-only with no FK to
`users`. It tracks aggregate persona-conversion shape, nothing else.

### I-3. Default copy is the SAME copy that exists today

The existing `Hero.tsx` lives unchanged. `PersonaAwareHero` ALWAYS
renders the existing Hero copy when persona resolves to `'default'`.
Any visual regression in the default state means the persona layer
broke unrelated logic — escalate.

Rationale: SEO and ad campaigns drove inbound to specific copy. We
preserve that copy for the segment of traffic that doesn't carry a
persona signal. Persona variants are upside; default is hold-harmless.

### I-4. Persona content is in code, not a CMS

`personaContent.ts` is a typed const map. Each persona variant must
type-check against the same `HeroContent` interface so a missing
field cannot ship. No headless CMS, no Sanity, no Strapi for this
WO — adding a content service is a separate decision with its own
operational burden (cost, latency, downtime, schema drift).

Editing a persona's copy is a code change with the standard PR
review path. This is intentional: the words are the product.

### I-5. Lab-notebook background is CSS-only

The lab-notebook ruled-paper aesthetic ships as `repeating-linear-gradient`
and `linear-gradient` layers driven by CSS custom properties — no
image, no base64, no SVG. Per the strategic doc:

- horizontal blue ruling: `rgba(140, 171, 217, 0.08)` default
- vertical red margin: `rgba(167, 51, 43, 0.15)` default
- line spacing: 40px
- margin position: 80px from left
- line thickness: 1px

Operators can tune these via the `:root` custom properties; no
component code touches the math.

### I-6. Contrast and dark-mode typography are non-negotiable

Default body text uses `#E0E0E0` (off-white, not `#FFFFFF`) on
`#0A0E1A` (the existing `r1-bg`). Contrast ratio: 13.5:1 — well
above WCAG AA (4.5:1) and AAA (7:1). The lab-notebook overlay must
not reduce effective contrast below 7:1 — operators tune the line
opacity if a new color combination drops below that. Test page is
`/landing-contrast-check.html` (dev only, hidden from prod nav).

Font weights shift +100 in dark mode per the strategic doc:
`font-weight: 500` for body, `600` for headings. Do not override
this with arbitrary `font-weight: 300` or `400` "for elegance" —
the dark-mode legibility research that motivated the shift is in
`docs/WO-V_VISUAL_DESIGN_NOTES.md`.

### I-7. Persona resolution is deterministic for the same inputs

Given identical (search params, referrer, pathname), `personaResolver.resolve()`
returns the same persona id every time. This is testable, and the
test suite asserts it for the documented input combinations.

Why this matters: a non-deterministic resolver would mean the same
user reloading the same URL sees different copy — a bizarre user
experience and an analytics nightmare.

### I-8. SessionStorage cache is opt-in and TTL'd

The resolver caches the resolved persona in `sessionStorage` under
key `r1.persona` so a user who arrives at `/` from `r/UFOs`, navigates
to `/pricing`, and returns to `/`, still sees the UAP-tuned copy on
return — without having to re-detect (the referrer is gone by then).

Cache TTL is the browser session (sessionStorage clears on tab close).
No localStorage. No cross-tab sync. If the user opens a new tab from
a non-persona referrer, that tab's resolver returns `default` — which
is correct, because their state of mind in the new tab is different.

### I-9. WO-W composition: the animated hero is a LAYER over WO-V

When WO-W ships the Framer Motion + Aceternity beam animation, it
mounts INSIDE `LabNotebookCanvas` and INSIDE `PersonaAwareHero`. The
animation reads `r1.persona` from `runtime context` (provided by the
PersonaAwareHero via React context) to choose beam color palette
per persona (OSINT = cyan/blue, UAP = purple/red, academic = teal/gold,
patent = amber/green, default = the existing accent).

Per Rule 25 I-9 reasoning by analogy: visual variants per persona are
the WO-W responsibility, not WO-V. Don't put beam colors in
`personaContent.ts`.

### I-10. Tests must fail without the fix (Cursor rule 16)

Persona resolver tests must:

- Assert each documented (query param, UTM, referrer, path) → persona
  mapping. Revert the resolver to always-return-default — the tests
  must fail.
- Assert deterministic output: same inputs → same persona, called 10×
  in a row.
- Assert sessionStorage cache hit returns the cached value WITHOUT
  re-detecting (mock `document.referrer` to throw — should not throw).
- Assert SSR safety: when `window` is undefined, resolve returns
  `'default'` without throwing.

## Pre-commit check

- [ ] `grep -rn "fetch\|XMLHttpRequest\|axios" frontend/src/components/landing/persona/` — must be zero hits. The resolver is wire-silent.
- [ ] `grep -rn "document.referrer" frontend/src/components/landing/persona/` — at most one hit, in `personaResolver.ts`. No copy-paste of referrer reading.
- [ ] If you added a new persona id, you added its content in `personaContent.ts`, its detection rules in `personaResolver.ts`, and its tests in `personaResolver.test.ts` — all in the same commit.
- [ ] If you changed the lab-notebook CSS variables, you re-ran the contrast check at `/landing-contrast-check.html` (dev page) and confirmed the body-text combination is ≥ 7:1.
- [ ] If you added analytics for a new persona, the backend writer
  validates the persona id against an enum (no free-text writes to
  the analytics table).
